Don't look at me like that.  I typed in the name of my guy on Kingdom
of Loathing, chose to get a random character and this is what I ended
up with.  <_<

Anyway, I had a fairly easy time even early on.  I found a couple
bonesfiles, one of which had another set of bones in it, from which I
got a silver dragon scale mail, cloak of magic resistance and plenty of
rings (including free action and slow digestion, which I used almost
till the end,) wands and potions.  Oh yeah, and a tinning kit, which I
got plenty of use out of.  Speed boots from a shop somewhere rounded
out my midgame equipment.  Naturally, I had a devil of a time finding
identify scrolls, but with the help of the magic lamp I found on a
bonesfile I was able to get by (I didn't identify it all until much
later, and that master mind flayer in Gehennom didn't help much
either.)  I didn't get a big room, and Sokoban was rather far down,
around level 10, but that was okay because I got a bag of holding,
which was quite a relief.
After making a big mess around an altar with some monster-creating
stuff, I ended up with Mjollnir, which was a welcome change from my +1
Excalibur (dipped).  Found gauntlets of power not much later--super
good.  I felt ready to take anything on, but my quest leader didn't
agree.
I came back one level later and made a couple rather stupid
blunders--first, when I was jumping into water to blank some potions
and scrolls, I dropped everything (useful) that could be damaged by
water--except my bag of holding.  I lost all my identify and what I
think were a couple of full healings that way.  At least I was able to
get enough holy water to last me the whole game.  Second, I lost my
speed boots to some lava.  If only I'd had enough enchant armor to
damageproof that too.  Oh well, the Quest nemesis ate wand of death
anyway.
Having a good bit of money from my pet silver dragon's antics, I picked
up some protection (down to -4) and made my way to the castle.  Froze
the moat, blew open the drawbridge with a wand of striking and let
myself in.  Thinking how much fun a ring of conflict would be right
now, I made a terrible mess out of the throne room (my dragon opted to
stay outside--guess some of the items sitting on the gate or in the
hall were cursed.)  I quickly found the wand of wishing and established
my stash in the same room (yes, I hadn't been using one before)--I'd
forgotten what it was like to be unburdened.  I immediately wished back
my speed boots and got a magic marker.  I immediately wrote some
blessed charging and then started buffing my equipment, ending up with
a blessed rustproof +6 Mjollnir, and several pluses and erodeproofing
(where applicable) on my SDSM, CoMR, gauntlets of power and speed
boots.  Interestingly enough, I still had on my blessed +1 etched
helmet from some guard or soldier (brought this to the endgame), and my
original, albeit burned-up, small shield.  I decided to toss the shield
and two-weapon with a silver saber (soon blessed +5) instead, and went
on a few forays into Gehennom getting expertise in doing so.
Unfortunately, on one such foray my dragon got polymorphed into
something else--what exactly it was escapes me at the moment--and died.
I found my second blessed magic lamp somewhere in Gehennom, and decided
to keep it for light.  I also found a wand of polymorph and had fun
polypiling the junk lying around the castle until I found a shirt (it
took one scroll of charging and one iron golem.)  The Hawaiian shirt
soon became fireproof and +3.
I made it to the bottom of Gehennom without much of a problem--never
did see Demogorgon, and the other demon lords posed little threat to my
~200 HP, low AC (-31 IIRC,) halved physical damage received and
veritable slew of resistances.  Well, maybe Juiblex, but I had a wand
of digging handy, so I was fine.  I bribed one demon lord--I forget
who--with a whopping 100something gold.  I also saw a few nurses, but
didn't tarry too long with them because of the environment, and ended
up killing and tinning most of them.  I was quite glad not to see any R
or L (mostly because I had blessed genocided them; unfortunately, I
didn't have enough uncursed genocide to get rid of master mind flayers,
and one of them punked my memory.  Never did find a can of grease.)
The Wizard and his cronies managed to get my HP below 130, but
ultimately fell.  On the way up, I got teleported back an annoying
number of times, and some of my stuff got cursed (thankfully not my bag
of holding--I didn't bring holy water down with me--but my silver saber
did get it, and I dropped it.)
I made a quick stop at the castle to get my final inventory in
order--put all non-essentials away, took my holy water (I blessed my 8
potions of full healing first,) re-blessed whatever needed to be, got
all my wands of digging out and some gold detection.  I left my pickaxe
and tinning kit behind, but took the tins of nurse meat (the homemade
Lord Surtur meat had to stay behind, unfortunately.)  I elected not to
bring my 80,000 gold (that stuff is heavy, even in a blessed bag of
holding) and just took my sizable gem collection with me.
When I got to the Planes, I realized I didn't have a way to get myself
confused, but some monsters found the portals for me, so I pulled
through just fine.  I kept my trusty (if rusty) ring of levitation on
throughout most of the whole thing, but switched to slow digestion upon
reaching the Astral Plane.  Something blinded me while I was there, and
I looked around with my telepathy.  Oh, Angel of Tyr, the right-hand
altar must be it.  Of course I neglected to check if the other altars
also had Angels of Tyr, and found myself at the neutral altar.  Oops.
I tried the center one next and it was chaotic.  By the time I hacked
my way to the left-hand temple, both my wands of death were empty and I
had used a couple full heals, but I was safe and still had plenty of
pep (read: amulet of life saving and more full heals sitting in my
bag.)  I closed and locked the door behind me, walked up to the priest
and #chatted him up to make absolutely sure I was in the right place.
Two bits for an ale, not that that would do much good here.  Okay,
#o--wait, I whistled in my guardian angel first.  Made sure the amulet
I had was named REAL, and then off went the bearded battle maiden to
awesomeness.  Success!

I ended up with over 500,000 points.  My vanquished list almost hit
3,000, too.  I was level 20.



